    "message": {
     "content": "### The Short Verdict\n**Do not accept this offer as-is, but do not walk away yet. You should negotiate aggressively.**\n\nYou were recruited passively, you are happy at Meta, and you have strong momentum and social capital where you are. Leaving a stable, high-paying IC6 role for a lateral move requires a clear financial premium to justify the risk and \"onboarding tax.\" Right now, this offer provides only a minor (~5%) bump on an annualized basis, actually cuts your base salary, and offers $0 sign-on. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Breaking Down the Real Numbers\n\n#### **Your Current Meta Comp (IC6):**\n* **Base:** $240,000\n* **Bonus (15%):** $36,000\n* **Equity:** ~$250,000/yr (with ~$200k in unvested RSUs in the next 12 months)\n* **Total Annual Comp:** **~$526,000**\n\n---\n\n#### **Google Offer (L6):**\n* **Base:** $235,000 *(lower than Meta; range is $183k\u2013$271k)*\n* **Bonus (20% target):** $47,000\n* **Equity:** $1,080,000 over 4 years\n* **Sign-on:** $0\n\n#### **Understanding Google's Equity Structure:**\nGoogle typically vests new-hire equity on a **front-loaded schedule** (standard is **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**):\n* **Year 1:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k stock = **$638,400**\n* **Year 2:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k stock = **$638,400**\n* **Year 3:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $237.6k stock = **$519,600** *(drops below your current Meta comp)*\n* **Year 4:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $129.6k stock = **$411,600** *(steep drop)*\n* **4-Year Annualized Average:** **~$552,000/yr** ($235k base + $47k bonus + $270k avg stock)\n\nWhile Year 1 and Year 2 look strong on paper due to front-loading, **the annualized compensation is only ~$26,000/year higher than what you make today.** While Google refreshers stack annually (vesting evenly at 25%/year), performance-dependent refreshers should not be counted on to fix an initial offer that isn't compelling enough from day one.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why This Offer Needs Negotiation\n1. **Base Salary is a Step Down:** It is lower than your current $240,000 base, even though the posted band goes up to $271,000.\n2. **No Sign-On Bonus:** Leaving Meta means walking away from unvested equity and resetting your bonus cycle. A $0 sign-on is standard for an initial offer, but abnormal for a closed deal at L6.\n3. **Your Leverage:** Because you were sourced passively and are content where you are, your willingness to walk away is your greatest negotiating asset.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Your Counter-Offer Strategy & Target Numbers\n\nAt Google L6 in the Bay Area, competitive compensation packages reach **$580k\u2013$650k+ annualized** (or $1.2M\u2013$1.4M total equity). \n\n| Component | Current Offer | Your Counter Target | Why |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Becomes a true increase over Meta and sits comfortably in the upper quartile of the $183k\u2013$271k band. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,280,000 \u2013 $1,350,000** | Lifts annualized equity to ~$320k\u2013$337k/yr, protecting against the Year 3/4 drop. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Compensates for the unvested stock left on the table at Meta and makes Year 1 clearly superior. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Communicate Your Counter\n\nSchedule a call with the recruiter and use this framing:\n\n> *\"I really enjoyed meeting the team and I'm excited about the scope of the L6 role in SF. However, looking at the compensation package as a whole, it doesn't quite make sense for me to leave my current position.*\n> \n> *As you know, I wasn't actively looking. At Meta, my current annualized compensation is roughly $526k, with a higher base salary of $240k and strong unvested equity vesting over the next year. On an annualized basis, this offer is essentially flat compared to where I am once we factor in the Year 3/4 equity taper.*\n> \n> *For me to justify walking away from my current project, unvested equity, and team momentum, we'd need to get the package to:*\n> * *A base salary of **$255,000**,*\n> * *An initial equity grant of **$1.3M**, and*\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$80,000** to offset what I'm leaving behind.*\n> \n> *If we can get the package to those numbers, I would be thrilled to sign immediately.\"*\n\n### Next Steps:\n* **If they meet your numbers (or meet you halfway, e.g., $1.2M stock + $60k sign-on + $250k base):** You will be stepping into an offer worth **~$600k+ annualized / ~$700k+ in Year 1**, making the switch financially worthwhile.\n* **If they refuse to move significantly:** Politely decline and stay at Meta. You lose nothing by holding out for a top-of-band package.",
