    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict\n**Financially, this revised offer is strong and worth taking.** \n\nWhile they did not give you a cash sign-on bonus, they shifted that value directly into equity (an extra **$200,000 in RSUs**), which achieves virtually the same cash-flow outcome in your first two years. \n\nIf you like the team, product area, and scope, **you should accept the offer.** If you are lukewarm on the team or scope, you can comfortably walk away knowing you extracted the maximum Google was willing to give.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Updated Math\n\nWith Google\u2019s standard front-loaded vesting schedule (**33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**), here is how your compensation looks:\n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Revised Google Offer (L6) | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | **$240,000** | Flat |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | **$48,000** (20%) | +$12,000/yr |\n| **Year 1 Equity** | ~$200,000 | **$422,400** (33% of $1.28M) | +$222,400 |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **$710,400** | **+$184,400 (+35%)** |\n| **Year 2 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **$710,400** | **+$184,400 (+35%)** |\n| **4-Yr Annual Average**| **~$526,000** | **~$608,000** | **+$82,000/yr (+15.6%)** |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why This is a Win (Even Without the Sign-On)\n\n1. **They fully offset your Year 1 Meta unvested stock:** You were worried about walking away from ~$200k of Meta stock vesting over the next 12 months. In Year 1 alone at Google, your equity vest will be **$422,400** (more than double what you\u2019d vest at Meta).\n2. **$370,000+ extra over 2 years:** Over your first 24 months, you will take home roughly **$1.42M** at Google vs. **$1.05M** at Meta.\n3. **The Year 3/4 Cliff is Protected by Refreshes:** \n   * In Year 3, your base grant equity drops to 22% ($281.6k), making TC ~$570k.\n   * However, after two performance cycles at Google, you will have received two annual equity refresher grants (which vest evenly at 25%/yr). An average L6 PM refresh is ~$90k\u2013$130k/yr. Stacking those will keep your Year 3 and Year 4 total comp well above **$620,000+**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. How to Make Your Final Decision\n\nBecause the compensation is now firmly in the top tier for a Google L6 PM, your decision should come down to career and lifestyle factors:\n\n#### **You should ACCEPT if:**\n* **You want a massive short-term liquidity boost:** Earning $710k/yr for the next two years accelerates savings, investments, or real estate goals significantly.\n* **You like the Google product scope:** The team, manager, and domain in San Francisco interest you as much as or more than your current Meta scope.\n* **You want Google brand/diversity on your resume:** Having both Meta IC6 and Google L6 PM credentials opens doors to VP/Head of Product roles anywhere in tech.\n* **Work-Life Balance matters:** While org-dependent, Google PM roles generally offer a more sustainable pace and lower churn pressure than Meta's PSC performance cycles.\n\n#### **You should WALK AWAY (Stay at Meta) if:**\n* **You have strong promotion momentum to IC7 at Meta:** An IC7 promotion at Meta will push your comp into the $750k\u2013$900k+ range faster than an L6-to-L7 promo at Google (where promotions are notoriously slower).\n* **The Google team/project feels risky or bureaucratic:** If you are currently working on a tier-1, high-visibility product at Meta and the Google role is in a maintenance or slow-growth org, the lateral onboarding risk may not be worth it.\n\n---\n\n### Final Recommendation\n\n**Take the offer.** You negotiated a **$200,000 equity increase and a base salary match**, taking your initial Year 1 comp from ~$638k up to **~$710k**. For a lateral move, this is a top-bracket Google L6 package.",
